<GuideLayout
  title="Why Some Activewear Factories Fail to Deliver Consistent Quality"
  type="Manufacturing Guide"
  description="Learn why activewear factories struggle with consistent quality, including fabric variation, weak pattern control, rushed sampling, poor QC and unclear buyer approvals."
>
  <p>Consistent activewear quality is not created by inspection alone. It depends on fabric control, pattern accuracy, sewing stability, measurement discipline, logo approval, packing standards and clear communication before bulk production starts.</p>

  <h2>Quick Answer</h2>
  <p>Factories usually fail at consistency when they lack clear material standards, repeatable production processes, trained QC staff, stable sewing lines or documented buyer approvals.</p>

  <h2>Common Reasons Quality Becomes Unstable</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Fabric lots change between sampling and bulk production.</li>
    <li>Patterns are adjusted informally without version control.</li>
    <li>Measurements are checked too late in the production cycle.</li>
    <li>Stitch tension, seam allowance and machine settings are not standardized.</li>
    <li>Logo placement, label position or packaging details are approved verbally instead of visually.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why Activewear Is Harder Than Basic Apparel</h2>
  <p>Activewear must stretch, recover, cover, support and move with the body. A small change in GSM, spandex percentage, seam construction or size grading can affect comfort, opacity and fit. This is why activewear factories need stronger fabric knowledge and production discipline.</p>

  <h2>How Buyers Can Reduce Risk</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Approve fabric composition, GSM, color, hand feel and shrinkage before cutting.</li>
    <li>Confirm a sealed sample before bulk production.</li>
    <li>Use measurement tolerances for each size.</li>
    <li>Request in-line inspection photos, not only final product photos.</li>
    <li>Confirm packing method, carton marks and final quantity before shipment.</li>
  </ul>

